{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2C4znFKQsL5PR6GL9r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q9NbKy57ggDm8Lsj3axAiW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/PLAN.md","limit":360},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2115,"cache_read_input_tokens":22521,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2115},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2adef66c-4a6d-4909-bb60-db906fac5610","uuid":"f36b2a4c-6e9b-48f2-a260-8e5eb69a6980","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:29:17.897Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2C4juDPZ3vbzfWNJin","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Q9NbKy57ggDm8Lsj3axAiW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/PLAN.md","limit":360}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q9NbKy57ggDm8Lsj3axAiW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Ice Hoc-Kaboom\n2\t\n3\t**The build plan for the game TASK.md describes** (source: ice hockey, over-the-top arcade style). Read TASK.md first, then\n4\tthis file whole. Everything here is required unless a line says otherwise. Numbers are starting values: they are\n5\texposed as dev-mode sliders (section 15.3), and the build tunes them.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. The game at a glance\n10\t\n11\t| | |\n12\t|---|---|\n13\t| **Title** | **Ice Hoc-Kaboom** (logo text `ICE HOC-KABOOM`; use exactly \"Ice Hoc-Kaboom\" wherever the name is written) |\n14\t| **Deliverable** | `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816.html` in this folder (the folder holding TASK.md) |\n15\t| **Dev folder** | `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816/` in this folder |\n16\t| **Also in this folder** | `exit-interview.json` (TASK.md's shape), beside the HTML |\n17\t| **Genre** | Arcade sports: 3-on-3 ice hockey with goalies. A fight minigame sits inside it, and a story campaign (the Cup Run) frames it. Party versus for 1–4 players, local or online through AI Hub |\n18\t| **Perspective** | A 2.5D TV broadcast camera: the engine's `threequarter` ground view (pitch 55°) over a rink that runs left–right and tracks the puck. An `Overhead` option uses the engine's `topdown` view. An ice-level `brawler` view takes over for fights, instant replays, player intros and celebrations |\n19\t| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package, full engine (all modules), patched as section 4 lists |\n20\t| **Look** | The engine's native look: PS1-era pixel art (`res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`) and HD Humanoid rigs, plus new hockey gear, rinks, crowds and effects |\n21\t| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `shared-world` mode, 1–4 seats |\n22\t\n23\t**The core loop, in one sentence (put it at the top of the game's source):** skate, dangle and rip the puck past the\n24\tgoalie while you hammer rivals through the glass; fill your team's BOOM meter to go KABOOM; win the scrap when the\n25\tgloves drop; outscore them before the final horn.\n26\t\n27\t**Pillars** (judge every feature against these):\n28\t1. **It plays like hockey.** Skating carries momentum and stops in a spray of snow. The puck rims the curved boards.\n29\t   Passes land tape to tape, one-timers are a thing, and goalies make real saves with reflexes, positioning and rebounds.\n30\t2. **Over the top.** Checks shatter the glass, gloves drop, KABOOM mode turns the slapshot into a fireball that blows the\n31\t   goalie into his own net, and fans throw power-ups and octopi onto the ice. The goal horn is huge.\n32\t3. **Hockey culture and its voice.** The old barn, the arena organ, the ice resurfacer (our \"Flood Wagon\"), chirping,\n33\t   the three stars, hats for a hat trick, the handshake line. A broadcast booth that never shuts up, in hockey's own\n34\t   jargon.\n35\t4. **10-foot, controller first.** A DualSense on a TV. Keyboard and mouse are just as native. Prompts show the device\n36\t   used last.\n37\t\n38\t**What the demo contains** (it is a complete vertical slice, every feature shown):\n39\t- **Cup Run**, the story: 5 chapters, 5 arenas and 5 rival teams, with cutscenes, one choice per chapter, 3 stars per\n40\t  chapter, a boss final and an end sequence with credits.\n41\t- **Exhibition**: any 2 of 6 teams in any of 5 arenas, 1–4 local players on any sides, options and unlockable cheats.\n42\t- **Practice**: 7 drills from Coach Marge that teach every mechanic.\n43\t- **Online**: AI Hub sessions for 1–4 players, one period, versus.\n44\t- **Records, Settings, About/Credits**. **Attract mode** on every title and menu screen. **Dev mode** from the pause menu.\n45\t\n46\t### Title pick (as TASK.md says)\n47\tI brainstormed 50 playful riffs on \"ice hockey\" silently, then rolled a real random number with\n48\t`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`. It came up **35: \"Ice Hoc-Kaboom\"** (\"hockey\" with an\n49\texplosion at the end, which is also the name of the game's signature mechanic). The file and folder names follow the\n50\tpattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]`, with the title's spaces dropped and SOURCE written `IceHockey`.\n51\t\n52\t---\n53\t\n54\t## 1. Deliverables, folders and working rules\n55\t\n56\t```\n57\t./TASK.md  ./STAGE.md  ./PLAN.md                 (given)\n58\t./IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816.html   (the game: one self-contained file)\n59\t./exit-interview.json                            (TASK.md's shape, checked to parse)\n60\t./IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816/      (every dev file)\n61\t    engine/          the handoff package, copied whole (my-3d2dge.js patched; API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, tools/, docs/, kits/)\n62\t    vendor/          session.js (AI Hub client 1.4.0, verbatim), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (test only)\n63\t    src/             the game, one file per module (section 5.2), concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n64\t    tests/           run.mjs (the one test command) and its suites (section 18)\n65\t    tools/build.mjs  writes the single HTML (and build/game.js for the headless tests)\n66\t    build/           build output for tests: game.js, dev.html\n67\t    hub/             served for the AI Hub harness: harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, game.html (a copy of the build)\n68\t    shots/           screenshots for review (not shipped)\n69\t    ENGINE-CHANGES.md   every change to the engine and why (section 4); its summary also goes in the About screen\n70\t    package.json     pins playwright 1.63.0 (copy the handoff's)\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t- **Getting the engine**: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git`, made **inside the dev\n74\t  folder** (e.g. `dev/_src/`), then copy `dist/handoff/` (4.9 MB) to `dev/engine/` and delete the clone. For the hub,\n75\t  use a sparse checkout, because the whole repo is very large (a full clone passed 780 MB before I stopped it):\n76\t  `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git _hub && cd _hub && git sparse-checkout set lib/hub`,\n77\t  then copy `lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js,README.md}` and `lib/hub/examples/arena.html` (the\n78\t  shared-world example) to `dev/vendor/`, and delete the clone.\n79\t- **This machine's `/tmp` is a small tmpfs that was 98% full while I planned.** Never clone or write there. Before you\n80\t  run Playwright, Chromium or npm, set `TMPDIR` to `dev/.tmp/` and create that folder.\n81\t- **Other agents share this machine.** Read only your own files. Serve with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`\n82\t  (the OS picks the port; parse it from the output), and fetch the page once to confirm it is this game: the\n83\t  `<title>` must contain \"Ice Hoc-Kaboom\". Stop only the processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`,\n84\t  `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n85\t- **Single-file build** (`node tools/build.mjs`) writes the HTML in this order:\n86\t  `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then **`<script>` + vendor/session.js verbatim +\n87\t  `</script>` as the first script** (the hub requires it right after `<meta charset>`), then the viewport meta,\n88\t  `<title>Ice Hoc-Kaboom</title>`, the hub metas (section 14.1), and the page style (black background, full-window\n89\t  canvas `#screen`, `image-rendering: pixelated`, `touch-action: none`). Then `</head><body>`, the boot splash markup\n90\t  from `E.bootSplash('ICE HOC-KABOOM')` (or the template's static `#e3-boot` div with the title changed), and\n91\t  `<canvas id=\"screen\">`. Then the engine inlined in a `<script>`, then the game inlined in a `<script>` (one IIFE).\n92\t  Nothing loads from the network.\n93\t- **No TODOs, placeholders or stubs** may ship. `tests/run.mjs` greps the built HTML's game script for `TODO`, `FIXME`,\n94\t  `placeholder`, `stub` and `lorem`, and fails if it finds one.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 2. Engine and hub facts the build relies on (verified while planning)\n99\t\n100\tRead `engine/API.md` whole, as the handoff README says. Grep `engine/API-INDEX.md` for names and read the\n101\t`AI_GUIDE.md` sections you need. The facts below are the ones this plan depends on.\n102\t\n103\t**Research done while planning** (for the About screen and the exit interview, together with whatever the build\n104\tadds): I read the engine's handoff package (README, API.md, the AI_GUIDE sections on standard features, views,\n105\tplayers, audio, the Shell, testing and the sports recipe, `kits/sports.js`, the headers of `modules/fight.js`,\n106\t`arcade.js` and `drive.js`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, and the engine source for the font, the pad glyphs, the input presets,\n107\tthe audio voice, the dialog voice, the Humanoid's weapons and `solvePose`, and `ik3`). I read the AI Hub protocol,\n108\tthe harness's checks and the shared-world example. No web research on hockey; the hockey culture here is general\n109\tknowledge.\n110\t\n111\t- **Start from the SPORTS kit** (`engine/kits/sports.js`, slice at line 159). It is a 2v2 game in `threequarter` with a\n112\t  TileMap cage, a Body ball that bounces and curls, passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` lobs, a held shot meter, a\n113\t  formation AI, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, radio commentary through a non-modal `E.Dialog`, and a `bot()` that\n114\t  plays a match through `game.input`. Copy its structure and replace everything hockey changes. The FIGHTING kit and\n115\t  `modules/fight.js` show the engine's fight conventions; the fight here is bespoke (section 7.11), not `E.Fighter`.\n116\t- **Fixed step**: `update(dt)` runs at 120 Hz with exact steps, so the same inputs and seed give the same game.\n117\t  `game.advance(s)` runs steps headless. `new E.Game()` is headless in Node (draw is skipped).\n118\t- **Views**: `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (80), `brawler` (25). `game.setView(id)`; a scene's\n119\t  `views: [...]` limits the cycle. The ground plane is x east, y south, z up; 16 units = 1 m = one tile.\n120\t- **Characters**: `new E.Humanoid({ size, build: 'heroic' | 'bulky', weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, colors })`;\n121\t  `rig.update(dt, state)` (vx, vy, facing, attack, pose, expr, down, flip, crouch, sprint, land…); `rig.J` holds\n122\t  the joints in the rig's frame. **`rig.solvePose` may be wrapped per rig** (`const base = rig.solvePose;\n123\t  rig.solvePose = function () { base.call(this); /* move J */ }`); this is how skating and stick-handling are added.\n124\t  `E.ik3(a, b, L1, L2, hint)` (two-bone IK) and `E.V3` (vector helpers) are exported. Weapons are drawn inside\n125\t  `_drawHD` and `_drawClassic` (search `o.weapon === 'staff'`, around lines 4536 and 4616); new gear goes there.\n126\t  `E.MOVES` has `jab cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow` for the fights. Poses: `cheer guard kneel crouch wave hips down\n127\t  die block`. `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom, turn })` draws live portraits.\n128\t- **The pixel font (5x7, `G57` near line 1320) has no PlayStation symbols.** `✕ ○ □ △` render as `?`, while the\n129\t  engine's `PAD_GLYPHS.playstation` uses them (line 1265). Patch the font (section 4). The engine's pause, settings and\n130\t  controls screens are HTML (`E.Shell`), so the symbols already show there.\n131\t- **Glyphs**: `game.input.glyphs = 'playstation' | 'xbox' | 'auto' | …`; `input.label(action, device)` gives a binding's\n132\t  label for that device; `input.lastDevice` is `'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'`. The engine's Controls\n133\t  screen already has a \"button symbols\" option.\n134\t- **Input**: presets are plain objects of action → codes (`KeyJ`, `Mouse0`, `Pad0`…`Pad16`; standard mapping: 0 ✕, 1 ○,\n135\t  2 □, 3 △, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 d-pad). `E.Input.DEFAULT` holds the menu\n136\t  keys. Also: `input.move()`, `pressed/down/released/buffered/consume`, `input.padAim` (right stick),\n137\t  `input.pointer()` (screen px), `game.mouseGround()` (a world point), `input.anyPressed()`, `input.rumble()`,\n138\t  `game.allowJoin`, `game.addPlayer({ pad } | { keys: 'right' })`, `E.Input.halfKeys`, and `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n139\t- **Standard features: don't rebuild them.** The pause menu (`menu: { items: [['Dev Mode', fn]], settings: [...],\n140\t  controls: {...}, quit }`), settings saved per game, rebinding, full screen, auto-pause, the corner buttons, the boot\n141\t  screen with a crash report, and `game.saves` / `E.store`. `new E.Shell(game, { theme, settings })` themes the HTML\n142\t  screens.\n143\t- **Audio**: `game.audio` is a chip synth: `sfx(voice)`, `define`, `music(song, { fade, sync })`, `layer`, `sting`,\n144\t  `tempo`, `duck`, `onBeat`, and `audio.ctx` (the Web Audio context, null until the first gesture). Its buses\n145\t  (`_master`, `_sfxBus`, `_musBus`) are private. Oscillator voices have no filter (only noise voices take `filter`/`q`).\n146\t  `E.Dialog` blips `talk.voice` (an sfx) on every second letter (line 8956).\n147\t- **Presentation tools**: `game.script(function* () {…})` coroutines, `game.tween`, `cam.frame(list, { margin, zoom })`,\n148\t  `game.freeze`, `game.shake`, `game.flash`, `game.hitFx`, `game.particles.{explosion, smoke, fire, sparks, bits, glints,\n149\t  dust, ring, text}`, `game.lights.add(x, y, z, r, i, { color })`, `game.weather = { kind: 'snow' }`, and scene\n150\t  transitions (`game.go(scene, data, { fade: 'iris' | 'wipe' | … })`).\n151\t- **Drive module**: `new E.Car({ x, y, angle, top, accel, brake, turn, grip })`, then\n152\t  `car.update(dt, map, { steer, gas, brake })`. It drives the Flood Wagon.\n153\t- **Asset store** (`engine/src/assets/sets/*.js`, loaded with `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS[id])`): it has `bench`,\n154\t  `bottle`, `bowl`, `snowman`, `campfire-*`, `sled-long`, `speaker`, `cup`, `flag` and pines (core library). Build a\n155\t  set of only the entries the game uses with `node engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` and inline it. Everything else is a new\n156\t  `E.SHAPES` entry (`docs/ASSETS.md` is the format: read it whole before writing one).\n157\t- **Tools**: `node engine/tools/check.mjs <page>[#scene]` plays a page in Chromium and reports errors, look notes and\n158\t  `game.debug.snapshot()`. `node engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs <page> --steps \"…\" --seed 1` records frames to judge\n159\t  motion. **AI Hub**: read `vendor/README.md` (the protocol) whole; the shared-world example is the hub repo's\n160\t  `lib/hub/examples/arena.html`.\n161\t\n162\t---\n163\t\n164\t## 3. Design decisions in brief (why this shape)\n165\t\n166\t- **3-on-3 + goalies** (the arcade standard), on a rink a little shorter than the NHL's: big plays, readable at 10 feet,\n167\t  enough bodies for passing plays and hits. No offside, icing or penalties: the only penalty is the fight loser's trip\n168\t  to the sin bin, which gives a power play.\n169\t- **Home teams wear dark jerseys and away teams white**, as in the pros. That is the clearest team read on a TV, and\n170\t  it is never color alone: the two teams differ in light versus dark.\n171\t- **One broadcast camera, never split screen**: like a TV game. Local players are marked by colored rings and P-tags.\n172\t- **Everything is data**: teams, players, arenas, chapters, cutscenes, objectives, commentary, power-ups and tuning,\n173\t  so designers can extend the game without touching systems.\n174\t\n175\t---\n176\t\n177\t## 4. Engine changes (make them in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, log each in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\n178\t\n179\t1. **Font**: add `✕ ○ □ △` to `G57` (5 wide, 7 tall). For example `✕: '.....|#...#|.#.#.|..#..|.#.#.|#...#|.....'`,\n180\t   `○: '.....|.###.|#...#|#...#|#...#|.###.|.....'`, `□: '.....|#####|#...#|#...#|#...#|#####|.....'`,\n181\t   `△: '.....|..#..|.#.#.|.#.#.|#...#|#####|.....'`. Why: PlayStation prompts on the canvas showed `?`.\n182\t2. **Hub controller rule**: in the gamepad poll, while button 8 (Create) is held, ignore button 9 (Options) entirely.\n183\t   Why: AI Hub reserves Create + Options. Harmless outside the hub.\n184\t3. **Hockey gear on Humanoid** (in both `_drawHD` and `_drawClassic`), all drawn in the rig's depth-sorted `ops`:\n185\t   - `weapon: 'stick'`: a shaft from `J.stickTop` through the lower hand to `J.blade` (the heel), then the blade along\n186\t     `J.bladeDir2` (6 u long, 1.2 u tall). Taped knob and tape on the blade; shaft in `colors.stick`.\n187\t     `weapon: 'gstick'` is the goalie's, with a wider paddle. The game's solvePose wrapper sets\n188\t     `J.stickTop / J.blade / J.bladeDir2` and the hands.\n189\t   - `hat: { style: 'hockey' }` is a rounded shell with a clear visor; `hat: { style: 'mask' }` is a goalie mask (a\n190\t     painted shell in `colors.hat` and `colors.trim`, plus cage bars over the face).\n191\t   - `skates: true` draws a boot in `colors.boot` and a 1-px steel blade under each foot.\n192\t   - Goalie gear: `pads: true` (lower legs 1.8x as wide, plus a pad rectangle in front of each shin in `colors.pads`),\n193\t     `blocker: true` (a rectangle at `handR`), `catcher: true` (a mitt disc with a pocket line at `handL`).\n194\t   - `number: '19'` and `letter: 'C'`: the jersey number drawn with the tiny font on the torso's back, or on the chest\n195\t     when the chest faces the camera; the letter on the chest. `stripes: '#111'` draws vertical stripes over the torso\n196\t     and sleeves (the referees).\n197\t   Why: the source's look rests on sticks, helmets, skates and goalie pads, and the rig had none of them.\n198\t4. **Audio**: (a) periodic waves `organ` (drawbar harmonics 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 at 1, .8, .55, .4, .2, .15) and `brass`\n199\t   (saw-like, odd-heavy) for `sfx` voices and music tracks; (b) `lp` / `lpTo` / `q` on oscillator voices (a lowpass\n200\t   biquad, optionally swept); (c) a public `audio.bus(name)` returning the `master`, `sfx` or `music` gain node, plus\n201\t   a `voice` bus (made in `_init`, routed to master) with `audio.voiceVolume`, applied in `applyVolume`. Why: the\n202\t   organ, the goal horn and gibberish voices need them.\n203\t5. **Dialog voice hook**: if `talk.voice` is a function, call `voice(ch, index, text, page)` for each newly shown letter\n204\t   instead of `sfx(voice)`. Why: each speaker babbles in their own voice.\n205\t6. Anything else changed along the way goes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md: what, where, why.\n206\t\n207\t---\n208\t\n209\t## 5. Architecture\n210\t\n211\t### 5.1 Runtime shape\n212\t- **One `E.Game`**: `new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'threequarter',\n213\t  input: HOCKEY_KEYS, menu: { items: [['Objectives & Info', () => Match.showInfo()], ['Restart Game', () =>\n214\t  Match.restart()], ['Dev Mode', () => Dev.open()]], settings: SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title' },\n215\t  autoPause: !HUB, id: 'ice-hoc-kaboom' })`. The closures run later, so modules defined further down are fine. In an\n216\t  AI Hub session (`HUB` true), build it with `items: []`, `quit: false` and `autoPause: false` (section 14).\n217\t- **Scenes** (`game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title: 'ICE HOC-KABOOM', steps } })`):\n218\t  `title`, `attract`, `sides` (exhibition side select), `setup` (exhibition teams, arena, options), `storymap`,\n219\t  `cutscene`, `match` (pausable: all hockey play, including the shootout and the story games), `flood` (pausable: the\n220\t  intermission minigame), `results`, `practice` (pausable: drills), `records`, `about`, `credits`, `session` (AI Hub:\n221\t  card → play → end) and `lab` (dev only: the animation lab).\n222\t- **Boot steps**: bake the floor TileMap of the first arena (the others lazily at match start, behind the boot or\n223\t  a \"loading\" card); register the synth voices and songs; build the logo sprites; validate the data tables (fail loudly\n224\t  in dev); load the save.\n225\t- **Deep links** (not in sessions; for tests and tools): `#attract`, `#story/<1-5>`, `#cutscene/<id>`,\n226\t  `#match/<home>/<away>/<arena>`, `#fight`, `#kaboom`, `#shootout`, `#flood`, `#practice/<1-7>`, `#results`,\n227\t  `#about`, `#records`, `#lab`, `#dev` (a match with the Dev panel open).\n228\t\n229\t### 5.2 Source modules (`src/`, concatenated in this order into one IIFE)\n230\tEach module is `const Name = (() => { … return { … }; })();`, the kit's style. Simulation modules never touch the\n231\tDOM, audio or particles directly: they emit events (5.4).\n232\t\n233\t| File | Module | Responsibility |\n234\t|---|---|---|\n235\t| `00-head.js` | – | `'use strict'`, `const E = My3D2dge`, the hub flags (`HUB = window.AIHub && AIHub.active`), the `game` instance, short-hands |\n236\t| `01-util.js` | `U` | math (angle helpers, segment–circle and segment–segment tests, quantize), RNG streams `RNG.sim` / `RNG.fx` (from `E.rng`), name sanitizer (NFD → ASCII; other characters → `?`) |\n237\t| `02-events.js` | `Bus` | `emit(type, data)`, `on(type, fn)`; a per-match event log |\n238\t| `03-tuning.js` | `T` | every tunable number: defaults, live values, slider metadata (`min`, `max`, `step`, `group`, `label`), `reset(key)`, `resetAll()`, persisted in `E.store('dev:tuning')` |\n239\t| `04-data-teams.js` | `TEAMS` | 6 teams (section 11.1): colors, logos (string sprites), players, ratings, voices, goal songs |\n240\t| `05-data-arenas.js` | `ARENAS` | 5 arenas (11.2): floor palette, boards style, ads, crowd, props, lights, weather, hazards, music, organ |\n241\t| `06-data-story.js` | `STORY` | chapters, cutscene scripts, choices, objectives, unlocks |\n242\t| `07-data-text.js` | `TEXT` | commentary lines by event, chirps, tips, three-stars lines, credits and About pages |\n243\t| `10-synth.js` | `Synth` | babble voices, goal horn, organ, crowd bed, skate noise; sfx defines; songs |\n244\t| `11-glyphs.js` | `Glyphs` | device-aware prompt chips: PS / Xbox button badges, keycaps and mouse icons, as sprites; `Glyphs.chip(g, action, x, y)`; `{pass}`-style tokens in text |\n245\t| `12-ui.js` | `UI` | 10-foot widgets: the tile menu, card carousel, list with sliders, buttons, big banners, toasts; focus by d-pad, stick, keys and mouse hover; click to pick; sounds |\n246\t| `20-rink.js` | `Rink` | geometry (rounded rectangle, lines, dots, creases, nets, panels, benches), collision queries, the floor TileMap, board/glass/net drawing, the scuff layer |\n247\t| `21-rigs.js` | `Rigs` | the SkaterRig, GoalieRig, RefRig and FanRig factories, solvePose overlays (skating, stick, goalie stances, fight holds), and accessories (dropped gloves, helmets, hats, octopus) |\n248\t| `22-skater.js` | `Skater` | the pure `skateStep(state, intent, params, dt)`, the action state machine, ratings → multipliers |\n249\t| `23-puck.js` | `Puck` | puck physics with substeps, possession, pickup and battles, deflections, posts, net and goal detection |\n250\t| `24-goalie.js` | `Goalie` | positioning, the save solver, save types, freeze or rebound, poke, the Kaboom blast, manual control (shootout) |\n251\t| `25-ai.js` | `AI` | team roles, decisions, steering, difficulty, item greed, fight AI, chirp AI |\n252\t| `26-match.js` | `Match` | the match: phases, faceoffs, clock, goals, periods, OT, shootout, final, stats, three stars, objectives, the sin bin; it owns the entities; `Match.step(dt)` |\n253\t| `27-hits.js` | `Hits` | checks, impulses, knockdowns, the glass, grudges |\n254\t| `28-fight.js` | `Fight` | the fight minigame |\n255\t| `29-boom.js` | `Boom` | BOOM meters and KABOOM mode |\n256\t| `30-items.js` | `Items` | fan throws, power-ups, hazards, arena hazards (thin ice, the mascot, the Discount Drop) |\n257\t| `31-camera.js` | `Cam` | the broadcast director: follow, lead, zoom, punch-in, fight, replay and intro framing |\n258\t| `32-replay.js` | `Replay` | the ring buffer, instant replay, the highlight reel for the credits |\n259\t| `33-talk.js` | `Talk` | the commentary booth, chirp bubbles, tips, subtitles, voices |\n260\t| `34-hud.js` | `Hud` | the score bug, meters, indicators, the radar, prompts, banners, the fight HUD, lower-thirds |\n261\t| `35-fx.js` | `Fx` | event → particles, lights, shakes, crowd reactions, sounds (the only place simulation events become sensory) |\n262\t| `36-crowd.js` | `Crowd` | fans in the stands (excitement, waves, signs, glass pounders), the organist, the bench players |\n263\t| `40-scene-title.js` … `56-scene-lab.js` | scenes | one file per scene in 5.1, numbered from 40 upward |\n264\t| `70-dev.js` | `Dev` | the dev panel (15.3), cheats, warps, debug overlays, the sound test, the animation lab |\n265\t| `71-drills.js` | `Drills` | the 7 practice drills, built from match primitives |\n266\t| `80-net.js` | `Net` | AI Hub connect, the own card, host and client sync (section 14) |\n267\t| `90-start.js` | – | the scene table, deep links, `game.start`, `game.debug`, `window.__game` |\n268\t\n269\t### 5.3 Entities (plain objects, owned by `Match`)\n270\t- `Skater`: `{ id, team, slot, def, x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, facing, state, stateT, turbo, hasPuck, ctrl: { kind: 'human' |\n271\t  'cpu' | 'remote', input, player, seat }, intent, items: {}, grudge: {}, stats, rig }`. States: `skate`, `stop`,\n272\t  `wind`, `shoot`, `pass`, `check`, `deke`, `poke`, `stun`, `down`, `getup`, `celebrate`, `faceoff`, `fight`, `bin`,\n273\t  `frozen` (pond plunge), `buried` (snowbank), `slip`.\n274\t- `Goalie`: `{ id, team, def, x, y, facing, state, save, size, blastT, rig }`. States: `ready`, `shuffle`, `butterfly`,\n275\t  `glove`, `blocker`, `stack`, `poke`, `cover`, `blasted`, `celebrate`, `manual`.\n276\t- `Puck`: `{ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, owner, lastTouch, prevTouch, onFire, inNet, out }`.\n277\t- `Ref`: one referee and one linesman. `Item`: `{ kind, x, y, vx, vy, t, z }`. `Hazard`: thin-ice patches, holes,\n278\t  the mascot, octopi, rats.\n279\t- `Intent` (what any controller produces, and what the network carries): `{ mx, my (−1..1), aim (world point or\n280\t  null), held: { shoot, lob, turbo }, press: { pass, shoot, deke, lob, chirp, switch } }`.\n281\t\n282\t### 5.4 The match step (fixed order; deterministic; `Match.step(dt)`)\n283\t1. **Intents**: humans from their inputs (or network packets on the host); the AI for CPU skaters (they re-think every\n284\t   0.12–0.3 s and steer in between).\n285\t2. **Phase logic**: faceoff timers, stoppages, celebrations, replays, fights, period ends.\n286\t3. **Actions**: start passes, shots, checks, dekes, pokes, gloves and chirps, which emit events.\n287\t4. **Movement**: `Skater.skateStep` for each skater, then skater–skater pushes, then board collisions.\n288\t5. **Puck**: if it has an owner, it follows the blade; otherwise it integrates in 4 substeps, ray-tested against boards,\n289\t   posts, nets, goalies, sticks and bodies. This emits shots, saves, posts, deflections, goals and outs.\n290\t6. **Goalies**: positioning and the save solver (5 and 6 interleave inside the substeps).\n291\t7. **Hits**: contacts in the check state, knockdowns, glass, grudges.\n292\t8. **Items and hazards**.\n293\t9. **Boom, Kaboom and sin-bin timers**.\n294\t10. **Clock**: period end, OT, shootout.\n295\t11. **Consumers** (they never feed back into the simulation): stats, objectives, `Talk`, `Fx`, `Crowd`, `Net`. These\n296\t    use `RNG.fx` only.\n297\t12. **Replay recorder**: every second step (60 Hz).\n298\t13. **Camera director**. 14. **Rig updates** from entity state.\n299\t\n300\t**RNG**: the simulation draws only from `RNG.sim`, seeded per match (story: chapter number × 1000 + attempt;\n301\texhibition: a seed from `Date.now()` taken at setup and shown in the Dev panel; session: from `AIHub.rng('sim')` on\n302\tthe host). Particles, crowd and commentary draw from `RNG.fx`.\n303\t\n304\t### 5.5 Debug handle (tests and tools read it)\n305\t`game.debug = { snapshot(), warp(target, opts), set(key, value), cheat(name, on), event(type, data), seed(n) }`.\n306\t`snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode, phase, period, clock, score, shots, puck: { x, y, z, owner }, skaters: [{ id,\n307\tteam, x, y, state, ctrl }], goalies: [{ x, y, state }], boom, kaboom, fight, items, sinBin, story: { chapter, stars,\n308\tstep }, attract: { segment, visited }, dev: { open }, net: { role, players }, errors: game.errors.length }`.\n309\t`window.__game = { game, scenes, Match, T, Dev, Net, STORY }`.\n310\t\n311\t---\n312\t\n313\t## 6. Controls (one game on every device)\n314\t\n315\t### 6.1 Actions and default bindings (`HOCKEY_KEYS`, built from `E.Input.DEFAULT`'s menu keys)\n316\t| Action | DualSense | Keyboard | Mouse | With the puck | Without the puck |\n317\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n318\t| move | left stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | – | skate | skate |\n319\t| `pass` | ✕ (Pad0) | J | right button | pass toward the aim | switch to the teammate nearest the puck; if a CPU teammate has the puck, **call for the pass** |\n320\t| `shoot` | □ (Pad2) | K | left button | tap: wrist shot; hold: slapshot wind-up, release to fire | body check |\n321\t| `deke` | ○ (Pad1) | L, Space | – | deke (spin move) | poke check |\n322\t| `lob` | △ (Pad3) | I | middle button | saucer pass | hold 0.5 s near a rival: drop the gloves (or accept a challenge) |\n323\t| `turbo` | R2 (Pad7), R1 (Pad5) | Shift | – | turbo (hold) | turbo (hold) |\n324\t| `chirp` | L1 (Pad4) | C | – | chirp | chirp |\n325\t| `switch` | L2 (Pad6) | Q | – | – | switch player (always) |\n326\t| `camera` | R3 (Pad11) | V | – | cycle Broadcast / Overhead | same |\n327\t| pause | Options | Esc, P | – | pause menu (engine) | |\n328\t\n329\t- **Why these keys**: WASD to move, the mouse to aim, left click to shoot and right click to pass, Shift to sprint\n330\t  (turbo) and Space to dodge (deke) are what PC players expect from sports and action games. J/K/L/I give\n331\t  keyboard-only players the engine's usual action cluster.\n332\t- **Aim**: with the mouse, when the pointer moved in the last 2 s, a reticle sits on the ice at `game.mouseGround()`:\n333\t  passes go to the teammate nearest it (within 80 u), else to the point; shots aim at the goal mouth where the line\n334\t  through the reticle meets it. On a pad, the right stick (`input.padAim`) aims the same way while deflected. Otherwise\n335\t  aim follows the move direction.\n336\t- **Context reuse**: in a fight, ✕ jab, □ haymaker, ○ duck, △ yank. In the shootout when defending, the stick slides\n337\t  the goalie, ✕ butterfly, □ poke, ○ dive. Flood Wagon: the stick steers toward that screen direction, R2/Shift is gas,\n338\t  ✕ honks.\n339\t- **Two on one keyboard**: `E.Input.halfKeys(HOCKEY_KEYS, 'left' | 'right')` gives P1 WASD + F G H R T Y and P2 the\n340\t  arrows + `. / , L ; '` and the numpad, each half taking pass, shoot, deke, lob, turbo, chirp in order. The side\n341\t  select screen shows both layouts with keycaps.\n342\t- **Controls screen names** (`menu.controls`): pass \"Pass / Switch\", shoot \"Shoot / Check\", deke \"Deke / Poke\",\n343\t  lob \"Saucer / Gloves\", turbo \"Turbo\", chirp \"Chirp\", switch \"Switch player\", camera \"Camera\". Hide the\n344\t  preset's unused `attack dash skill jump` (`false`).\n345\t\n346\t### 6.2 Prompts and glyphs\n347\t- `Glyphs.chip(g, action, x, y, { device })` draws the binding for the device used last (`input.lastDevice`, per\n348\t  player). **Gamepad**: a round dark badge with the PlayStation symbol in its color (✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red\n349\t  `#ff6b6b`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#4fe3a1`). With **Settings → Button symbols: Xbox**, the badge shows A, B, X or\n350\t  Y in green, red, blue or yellow. Shoulders and triggers are rounded rectangles (L1 R1 L2 R2, or LB RB LT RT).\n351\t  **Keyboard**: a keycap with the key's label. **Mouse**: a 7×10 mouse icon with the pressed button lit.\n352\t- **Settings → Button symbols** (`PlayStation` default, `Xbox`) sets `game.input.glyphs`. Keep it in sync both ways\n353\t  with the engine's own Controls option.\n354\t- Text with tokens (`\"Hold {shoot} for a slapshot\"`) is laid out by `Glyphs.text(g, str, x, y, opts)`, which replaces\n355\t  each `{action}` with a chip.\n356\t\n357\t---\n358\t\n359\t## 7. Systems (rules, numbers, behavior)\n360\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2adef66c-4a6d-4909-bb60-db906fac5610","uuid":"61e48bff-2ee0-43bd-ae9a-6c40968d6692","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:29:18.043Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Ice Hoc-Kaboom\n\n**The build plan for the game TASK.md describes** (source: ice hockey, over-the-top arcade style). Read TASK.md first, then\nthis file whole. Everything here is required unless a line says otherwise. Numbers are starting values: they are\nexposed as dev-mode sliders (section 15.3), and the build tunes them.\n\n---\n\n## 0. The game at a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | **Ice Hoc-Kaboom** (logo text `ICE HOC-KABOOM`; use exactly \"Ice Hoc-Kaboom\" wherever the name is written) |\n| **Deliverable** | `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816.html` in this folder (the folder holding TASK.md) |\n| **Dev folder** | `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816/` in this folder |\n| **Also in this folder** | `exit-interview.json` (TASK.md's shape), beside the HTML |\n| **Genre** | Arcade sports: 3-on-3 ice hockey with goalies. A fight minigame sits inside it, and a story campaign (the Cup Run) frames it. Party versus for 1–4 players, local or online through AI Hub |\n| **Perspective** | A 2.5D TV broadcast camera: the engine's `threequarter` ground view (pitch 55°) over a rink that runs left–right and tracks the puck. An `Overhead` option uses the engine's `topdown` view. An ice-level `brawler` view takes over for fights, instant replays, player intros and celebrations |\n| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package, full engine (all modules), patched as section 4 lists |\n| **Look** | The engine's native look: PS1-era pixel art (`res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`) and HD Humanoid rigs, plus new hockey gear, rinks, crowds and effects |\n| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `shared-world` mode, 1–4 seats |\n\n**The core loop, in one sentence (put it at the top of the game's source):** skate, dangle and rip the puck past the\ngoalie while you hammer rivals through the glass; fill your team's BOOM meter to go KABOOM; win the scrap when the\ngloves drop; outscore them before the final horn.\n\n**Pillars** (judge every feature against these):\n1. **It plays like hockey.** Skating carries momentum and stops in a spray of snow. The puck rims the curved boards.\n   Passes land tape to tape, one-timers are a thing, and goalies make real saves with reflexes, positioning and rebounds.\n2. **Over the top.** Checks shatter the glass, gloves drop, KABOOM mode turns the slapshot into a fireball that blows the\n   goalie into his own net, and fans throw power-ups and octopi onto the ice. The goal horn is huge.\n3. **Hockey culture and its voice.** The old barn, the arena organ, the ice resurfacer (our \"Flood Wagon\"), chirping,\n   the three stars, hats for a hat trick, the handshake line. A broadcast booth that never shuts up, in hockey's own\n   jargon.\n4. **10-foot, controller first.** A DualSense on a TV. Keyboard and mouse are just as native. Prompts show the device\n   used last.\n\n**What the demo contains** (it is a complete vertical slice, every feature shown):\n- **Cup Run**, the story: 5 chapters, 5 arenas and 5 rival teams, with cutscenes, one choice per chapter, 3 stars per\n  chapter, a boss final and an end sequence with credits.\n- **Exhibition**: any 2 of 6 teams in any of 5 arenas, 1–4 local players on any sides, options and unlockable cheats.\n- **Practice**: 7 drills from Coach Marge that teach every mechanic.\n- **Online**: AI Hub sessions for 1–4 players, one period, versus.\n- **Records, Settings, About/Credits**. **Attract mode** on every title and menu screen. **Dev mode** from the pause menu.\n\n### Title pick (as TASK.md says)\nI brainstormed 50 playful riffs on \"ice hockey\" silently, then rolled a real random number with\n`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`. It came up **35: \"Ice Hoc-Kaboom\"** (\"hockey\" with an\nexplosion at the end, which is also the name of the game's signature mechanic). The file and folder names follow the\npattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]`, with the title's spaces dropped and SOURCE written `IceHockey`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Deliverables, folders and working rules\n\n```\n./TASK.md  ./STAGE.md  ./PLAN.md                 (given)\n./IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816.html   (the game: one self-contained file)\n./exit-interview.json                            (TASK.md's shape, checked to parse)\n./IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174816/      (every dev file)\n    engine/          the handoff package, copied whole (my-3d2dge.js patched; API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, tools/, docs/, kits/)\n    vendor/          session.js (AI Hub client 1.4.0, verbatim), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (test only)\n    src/             the game, one file per module (section 5.2), concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n    tests/           run.mjs (the one test command) and its suites (section 18)\n    tools/build.mjs  writes the single HTML (and build/game.js for the headless tests)\n    build/           build output for tests: game.js, dev.html\n    hub/             served for the AI Hub harness: harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, game.html (a copy of the build)\n    shots/           screenshots for review (not shipped)\n    ENGINE-CHANGES.md   every change to the engine and why (section 4); its summary also goes in the About screen\n    package.json     pins playwright 1.63.0 (copy the handoff's)\n```\n\n- **Getting the engine**: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git`, made **inside the dev\n  folder** (e.g. `dev/_src/`), then copy `dist/handoff/` (4.9 MB) to `dev/engine/` and delete the clone. For the hub,\n  use a sparse checkout, because the whole repo is very large (a full clone passed 780 MB before I stopped it):\n  `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git _hub && cd _hub && git sparse-checkout set lib/hub`,\n  then copy `lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js,README.md}` and `lib/hub/examples/arena.html` (the\n  shared-world example) to `dev/vendor/`, and delete the clone.\n- **This machine's `/tmp` is a small tmpfs that was 98% full while I planned.** Never clone or write there. Before you\n  run Playwright, Chromium or npm, set `TMPDIR` to `dev/.tmp/` and create that folder.\n- **Other agents share this machine.** Read only your own files. Serve with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`\n  (the OS picks the port; parse it from the output), and fetch the page once to confirm it is this game: the\n  `<title>` must contain \"Ice Hoc-Kaboom\". Stop only the processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`,\n  `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n- **Single-file build** (`node tools/build.mjs`) writes the HTML in this order:\n  `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then **`<script>` + vendor/session.js verbatim +\n  `</script>` as the first script** (the hub requires it right after `<meta charset>`), then the viewport meta,\n  `<title>Ice Hoc-Kaboom</title>`, the hub metas (section 14.1), and the page style (black background, full-window\n  canvas `#screen`, `image-rendering: pixelated`, `touch-action: none`). Then `</head><body>`, the boot splash markup\n  from `E.bootSplash('ICE HOC-KABOOM')` (or the template's static `#e3-boot` div with the title changed), and\n  `<canvas id=\"screen\">`. Then the engine inlined in a `<script>`, then the game inlined in a `<script>` (one IIFE).\n  Nothing loads from the network.\n- **No TODOs, placeholders or stubs** may ship. `tests/run.mjs` greps the built HTML's game script for `TODO`, `FIXME`,\n  `placeholder`, `stub` and `lorem`, and fails if it finds one.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Engine and hub facts the build relies on (verified while planning)\n\nRead `engine/API.md` whole, as the handoff README says. Grep `engine/API-INDEX.md` for names and read the\n`AI_GUIDE.md` sections you need. The facts below are the ones this plan depends on.\n\n**Research done while planning** (for the About screen and the exit interview, together with whatever the build\nadds): I read the engine's handoff package (README, API.md, the AI_GUIDE sections on standard features, views,\nplayers, audio, the Shell, testing and the sports recipe, `kits/sports.js`, the headers of `modules/fight.js`,\n`arcade.js` and `drive.js`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, and the engine source for the font, the pad glyphs, the input presets,\nthe audio voice, the dialog voice, the Humanoid's weapons and `solvePose`, and `ik3`). I read the AI Hub protocol,\nthe harness's checks and the shared-world example. No web research on hockey; the hockey culture here is general\nknowledge.\n\n- **Start from the SPORTS kit** (`engine/kits/sports.js`, slice at line 159). It is a 2v2 game in `threequarter` with a\n  TileMap cage, a Body ball that bounces and curls, passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` lobs, a held shot meter, a\n  formation AI, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, radio commentary through a non-modal `E.Dialog`, and a `bot()` that\n  plays a match through `game.input`. Copy its structure and replace everything hockey changes. The FIGHTING kit and\n  `modules/fight.js` show the engine's fight conventions; the fight here is bespoke (section 7.11), not `E.Fighter`.\n- **Fixed step**: `update(dt)` runs at 120 Hz with exact steps, so the same inputs and seed give the same game.\n  `game.advance(s)` runs steps headless. `new E.Game()` is headless in Node (draw is skipped).\n- **Views**: `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (80), `brawler` (25). `game.setView(id)`; a scene's\n  `views: [...]` limits the cycle. The ground plane is x east, y south, z up; 16 units = 1 m = one tile.\n- **Characters**: `new E.Humanoid({ size, build: 'heroic' | 'bulky', weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, colors })`;\n  `rig.update(dt, state)` (vx, vy, facing, attack, pose, expr, down, flip, crouch, sprint, land…); `rig.J` holds\n  the joints in the rig's frame. **`rig.solvePose` may be wrapped per rig** (`const base = rig.solvePose;\n  rig.solvePose = function () { base.call(this); /* move J */ }`); this is how skating and stick-handling are added.\n  `E.ik3(a, b, L1, L2, hint)` (two-bone IK) and `E.V3` (vector helpers) are exported. Weapons are drawn inside\n  `_drawHD` and `_drawClassic` (search `o.weapon === 'staff'`, around lines 4536 and 4616); new gear goes there.\n  `E.MOVES` has `jab cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow` for the fights. Poses: `cheer guard kneel crouch wave hips down\n  die block`. `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom, turn })` draws live portraits.\n- **The pixel font (5x7, `G57` near line 1320) has no PlayStation symbols.** `✕ ○ □ △` render as `?`, while the\n  engine's `PAD_GLYPHS.playstation` uses them (line 1265). Patch the font (section 4). The engine's pause, settings and\n  controls screens are HTML (`E.Shell`), so the symbols already show there.\n- **Glyphs**: `game.input.glyphs = 'playstation' | 'xbox' | 'auto' | …`; `input.label(action, device)` gives a binding's\n  label for that device; `input.lastDevice` is `'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'`. The engine's Controls\n  screen already has a \"button symbols\" option.\n- **Input**: presets are plain objects of action → codes (`KeyJ`, `Mouse0`, `Pad0`…`Pad16`; standard mapping: 0 ✕, 1 ○,\n  2 □, 3 △, 4 L1, 5 R1, 6 L2, 7 R2, 8 Create, 9 Options, 10 L3, 11 R3, 12–15 d-pad). `E.Input.DEFAULT` holds the menu\n  keys. Also: `input.move()`, `pressed/down/released/buffered/consume`, `input.padAim` (right stick),\n  `input.pointer()` (screen px), `game.mouseGround()` (a world point), `input.anyPressed()`, `input.rumble()`,\n  `game.allowJoin`, `game.addPlayer({ pad } | { keys: 'right' })`, `E.Input.halfKeys`, and `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n- **Standard features: don't rebuild them.** The pause menu (`menu: { items: [['Dev Mode', fn]], settings: [...],\n  controls: {...}, quit }`), settings saved per game, rebinding, full screen, auto-pause, the corner buttons, the boot\n  screen with a crash report, and `game.saves` / `E.store`. `new E.Shell(game, { theme, settings })` themes the HTML\n  screens.\n- **Audio**: `game.audio` is a chip synth: `sfx(voice)`, `define`, `music(song, { fade, sync })`, `layer`, `sting`,\n  `tempo`, `duck`, `onBeat`, and `audio.ctx` (the Web Audio context, null until the first gesture). Its buses\n  (`_master`, `_sfxBus`, `_musBus`) are private. Oscillator voices have no filter (only noise voices take `filter`/`q`).\n  `E.Dialog` blips `talk.voice` (an sfx) on every second letter (line 8956).\n- **Presentation tools**: `game.script(function* () {…})` coroutines, `game.tween`, `cam.frame(list, { margin, zoom })`,\n  `game.freeze`, `game.shake`, `game.flash`, `game.hitFx`, `game.particles.{explosion, smoke, fire, sparks, bits, glints,\n  dust, ring, text}`, `game.lights.add(x, y, z, r, i, { color })`, `game.weather = { kind: 'snow' }`, and scene\n  transitions (`game.go(scene, data, { fade: 'iris' | 'wipe' | … })`).\n- **Drive module**: `new E.Car({ x, y, angle, top, accel, brake, turn, grip })`, then\n  `car.update(dt, map, { steer, gas, brake })`. It drives the Flood Wagon.\n- **Asset store** (`engine/src/assets/sets/*.js`, loaded with `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS[id])`): it has `bench`,\n  `bottle`, `bowl`, `snowman`, `campfire-*`, `sled-long`, `speaker`, `cup`, `flag` and pines (core library). Build a\n  set of only the entries the game uses with `node engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` and inline it. Everything else is a new\n  `E.SHAPES` entry (`docs/ASSETS.md` is the format: read it whole before writing one).\n- **Tools**: `node engine/tools/check.mjs <page>[#scene]` plays a page in Chromium and reports errors, look notes and\n  `game.debug.snapshot()`. `node engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs <page> --steps \"…\" --seed 1` records frames to judge\n  motion. **AI Hub**: read `vendor/README.md` (the protocol) whole; the shared-world example is the hub repo's\n  `lib/hub/examples/arena.html`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Design decisions in brief (why this shape)\n\n- **3-on-3 + goalies** (the arcade standard), on a rink a little shorter than the NHL's: big plays, readable at 10 feet,\n  enough bodies for passing plays and hits. No offside, icing or penalties: the only penalty is the fight loser's trip\n  to the sin bin, which gives a power play.\n- **Home teams wear dark jerseys and away teams white**, as in the pros. That is the clearest team read on a TV, and\n  it is never color alone: the two teams differ in light versus dark.\n- **One broadcast camera, never split screen**: like a TV game. Local players are marked by colored rings and P-tags.\n- **Everything is data**: teams, players, arenas, chapters, cutscenes, objectives, commentary, power-ups and tuning,\n  so designers can extend the game without touching systems.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Engine changes (make them in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, log each in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\n\n1. **Font**: add `✕ ○ □ △` to `G57` (5 wide, 7 tall). For example `✕: '.....|#...#|.#.#.|..#..|.#.#.|#...#|.....'`,\n   `○: '.....|.###.|#...#|#...#|#...#|.###.|.....'`, `□: '.....|#####|#...#|#...#|#...#|#####|.....'`,\n   `△: '.....|..#..|.#.#.|.#.#.|#...#|#####|.....'`. Why: PlayStation prompts on the canvas showed `?`.\n2. **Hub controller rule**: in the gamepad poll, while button 8 (Create) is held, ignore button 9 (Options) entirely.\n   Why: AI Hub reserves Create + Options. Harmless outside the hub.\n3. **Hockey gear on Humanoid** (in both `_drawHD` and `_drawClassic`), all drawn in the rig's depth-sorted `ops`:\n   - `weapon: 'stick'`: a shaft from `J.stickTop` through the lower hand to `J.blade` (the heel), then the blade along\n     `J.bladeDir2` (6 u long, 1.2 u tall). Taped knob and tape on the blade; shaft in `colors.stick`.\n     `weapon: 'gstick'` is the goalie's, with a wider paddle. The game's solvePose wrapper sets\n     `J.stickTop / J.blade / J.bladeDir2` and the hands.\n   - `hat: { style: 'hockey' }` is a rounded shell with a clear visor; `hat: { style: 'mask' }` is a goalie mask (a\n     painted shell in `colors.hat` and `colors.trim`, plus cage bars over the face).\n   - `skates: true` draws a boot in `colors.boot` and a 1-px steel blade under each foot.\n   - Goalie gear: `pads: true` (lower legs 1.8x as wide, plus a pad rectangle in front of each shin in `colors.pads`),\n     `blocker: true` (a rectangle at `handR`), `catcher: true` (a mitt disc with a pocket line at `handL`).\n   - `number: '19'` and `letter: 'C'`: the jersey number drawn with the tiny font on the torso's back, or on the chest\n     when the chest faces the camera; the letter on the chest. `stripes: '#111'` draws vertical stripes over the torso\n     and sleeves (the referees).\n   Why: the source's look rests on sticks, helmets, skates and goalie pads, and the rig had none of them.\n4. **Audio**: (a) periodic waves `organ` (drawbar harmonics 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 at 1, .8, .55, .4, .2, .15) and `brass`\n   (saw-like, odd-heavy) for `sfx` voices and music tracks; (b) `lp` / `lpTo` / `q` on oscillator voices (a lowpass\n   biquad, optionally swept); (c) a public `audio.bus(name)` returning the `master`, `sfx` or `music` gain node, plus\n   a `voice` bus (made in `_init`, routed to master) with `audio.voiceVolume`, applied in `applyVolume`. Why: the\n   organ, the goal horn and gibberish voices need them.\n5. **Dialog voice hook**: if `talk.voice` is a function, call `voice(ch, index, text, page)` for each newly shown letter\n   instead of `sfx(voice)`. Why: each speaker babbles in their own voice.\n6. Anything else changed along the way goes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md: what, where, why.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 Runtime shape\n- **One `E.Game`**: `new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'threequarter',\n  input: HOCKEY_KEYS, menu: { items: [['Objectives & Info', () => Match.showInfo()], ['Restart Game', () =>\n  Match.restart()], ['Dev Mode', () => Dev.open()]], settings: SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title' },\n  autoPause: !HUB, id: 'ice-hoc-kaboom' })`. The closures run later, so modules defined further down are fine. In an\n  AI Hub session (`HUB` true), build it with `items: []`, `quit: false` and `autoPause: false` (section 14).\n- **Scenes** (`game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title: 'ICE HOC-KABOOM', steps } })`):\n  `title`, `attract`, `sides` (exhibition side select), `setup` (exhibition teams, arena, options), `storymap`,\n  `cutscene`, `match` (pausable: all hockey play, including the shootout and the story games), `flood` (pausable: the\n  intermission minigame), `results`, `practice` (pausable: drills), `records`, `about`, `credits`, `session` (AI Hub:\n  card → play → end) and `lab` (dev only: the animation lab).\n- **Boot steps**: bake the floor TileMap of the first arena (the others lazily at match start, behind the boot or\n  a \"loading\" card); register the synth voices and songs; build the logo sprites; validate the data tables (fail loudly\n  in dev); load the save.\n- **Deep links** (not in sessions; for tests and tools): `#attract`, `#story/<1-5>`, `#cutscene/<id>`,\n  `#match/<home>/<away>/<arena>`, `#fight`, `#kaboom`, `#shootout`, `#flood`, `#practice/<1-7>`, `#results`,\n  `#about`, `#records`, `#lab`, `#dev` (a match with the Dev panel open).\n\n### 5.2 Source modules (`src/`, concatenated in this order into one IIFE)\nEach module is `const Name = (() => { … return { … }; })();`, the kit's style. Simulation modules never touch the\nDOM, audio or particles directly: they emit events (5.4).\n\n| File | Module | Responsibility |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-head.js` | – | `'use strict'`, `const E = My3D2dge`, the hub flags (`HUB = window.AIHub && AIHub.active`), the `game` instance, short-hands |\n| `01-util.js` | `U` | math (angle helpers, segment–circle and segment–segment tests, quantize), RNG streams `RNG.sim` / `RNG.fx` (from `E.rng`), name sanitizer (NFD → ASCII; other characters → `?`) |\n| `02-events.js` | `Bus` | `emit(type, data)`, `on(type, fn)`; a per-match event log |\n| `03-tuning.js` | `T` | every tunable number: defaults, live values, slider metadata (`min`, `max`, `step`, `group`, `label`), `reset(key)`, `resetAll()`, persisted in `E.store('dev:tuning')` |\n| `04-data-teams.js` | `TEAMS` | 6 teams (section 11.1): colors, logos (string sprites), players, ratings, voices, goal songs |\n| `05-data-arenas.js` | `ARENAS` | 5 arenas (11.2): floor palette, boards style, ads, crowd, props, lights, weather, hazards, music, organ |\n| `06-data-story.js` | `STORY` | chapters, cutscene scripts, choices, objectives, unlocks |\n| `07-data-text.js` | `TEXT` | commentary lines by event, chirps, tips, three-stars lines, credits and About pages |\n| `10-synth.js` | `Synth` | babble voices, goal horn, organ, crowd bed, skate noise; sfx defines; songs |\n| `11-glyphs.js` | `Glyphs` | device-aware prompt chips: PS / Xbox button badges, keycaps and mouse icons, as sprites; `Glyphs.chip(g, action, x, y)`; `{pass}`-style tokens in text |\n| `12-ui.js` | `UI` | 10-foot widgets: the tile menu, card carousel, list with sliders, buttons, big banners, toasts; focus by d-pad, stick, keys and mouse hover; click to pick; sounds |\n| `20-rink.js` | `Rink` | geometry (rounded rectangle, lines, dots, creases, nets, panels, benches), collision queries, the floor TileMap, board/glass/net drawing, the scuff layer |\n| `21-rigs.js` | `Rigs` | the SkaterRig, GoalieRig, RefRig and FanRig factories, solvePose overlays (skating, stick, goalie stances, fight holds), and accessories (dropped gloves, helmets, hats, octopus) |\n| `22-skater.js` | `Skater` | the pure `skateStep(state, intent, params, dt)`, the action state machine, ratings → multipliers |\n| `23-puck.js` | `Puck` | puck physics with substeps, possession, pickup and battles, deflections, posts, net and goal detection |\n| `24-goalie.js` | `Goalie` | positioning, the save solver, save types, freeze or rebound, poke, the Kaboom blast, manual control (shootout) |\n| `25-ai.js` | `AI` | team roles, decisions, steering, difficulty, item greed, fight AI, chirp AI |\n| `26-match.js` | `Match` | the match: phases, faceoffs, clock, goals, periods, OT, shootout, final, stats, three stars, objectives, the sin bin; it owns the entities; `Match.step(dt)` |\n| `27-hits.js` | `Hits` | checks, impulses, knockdowns, the glass, grudges |\n| `28-fight.js` | `Fight` | the fight minigame |\n| `29-boom.js` | `Boom` | BOOM meters and KABOOM mode |\n| `30-items.js` | `Items` | fan throws, power-ups, hazards, arena hazards (thin ice, the mascot, the Discount Drop) |\n| `31-camera.js` | `Cam` | the broadcast director: follow, lead, zoom, punch-in, fight, replay and intro framing |\n| `32-replay.js` | `Replay` | the ring buffer, instant replay, the highlight reel for the credits |\n| `33-talk.js` | `Talk` | the commentary booth, chirp bubbles, tips, subtitles, voices |\n| `34-hud.js` | `Hud` | the score bug, meters, indicators, the radar, prompts, banners, the fight HUD, lower-thirds |\n| `35-fx.js` | `Fx` | event → particles, lights, shakes, crowd reactions, sounds (the only place simulation events become sensory) |\n| `36-crowd.js` | `Crowd` | fans in the stands (excitement, waves, signs, glass pounders), the organist, the bench players |\n| `40-scene-title.js` … `56-scene-lab.js` | scenes | one file per scene in 5.1, numbered from 40 upward |\n| `70-dev.js` | `Dev` | the dev panel (15.3), cheats, warps, debug overlays, the sound test, the animation lab |\n| `71-drills.js` | `Drills` | the 7 practice drills, built from match primitives |\n| `80-net.js` | `Net` | AI Hub connect, the own card, host and client sync (section 14) |\n| `90-start.js` | – | the scene table, deep links, `game.start`, `game.debug`, `window.__game` |\n\n### 5.3 Entities (plain objects, owned by `Match`)\n- `Skater`: `{ id, team, slot, def, x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, facing, state, stateT, turbo, hasPuck, ctrl: { kind: 'human' |\n  'cpu' | 'remote', input, player, seat }, intent, items: {}, grudge: {}, stats, rig }`. States: `skate`, `stop`,\n  `wind`, `shoot`, `pass`, `check`, `deke`, `poke`, `stun`, `down`, `getup`, `celebrate`, `faceoff`, `fight`, `bin`,\n  `frozen` (pond plunge), `buried` (snowbank), `slip`.\n- `Goalie`: `{ id, team, def, x, y, facing, state, save, size, blastT, rig }`. States: `ready`, `shuffle`, `butterfly`,\n  `glove`, `blocker`, `stack`, `poke`, `cover`, `blasted`, `celebrate`, `manual`.\n- `Puck`: `{ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, owner, lastTouch, prevTouch, onFire, inNet, out }`.\n- `Ref`: one referee and one linesman. `Item`: `{ kind, x, y, vx, vy, t, z }`. `Hazard`: thin-ice patches, holes,\n  the mascot, octopi, rats.\n- `Intent` (what any controller produces, and what the network carries): `{ mx, my (−1..1), aim (world point or\n  null), held: { shoot, lob, turbo }, press: { pass, shoot, deke, lob, chirp, switch } }`.\n\n### 5.4 The match step (fixed order; deterministic; `Match.step(dt)`)\n1. **Intents**: humans from their inputs (or network packets on the host); the AI for CPU skaters (they re-think every\n   0.12–0.3 s and steer in between).\n2. **Phase logic**: faceoff timers, stoppages, celebrations, replays, fights, period ends.\n3. **Actions**: start passes, shots, checks, dekes, pokes, gloves and chirps, which emit events.\n4. **Movement**: `Skater.skateStep` for each skater, then skater–skater pushes, then board collisions.\n5. **Puck**: if it has an owner, it follows the blade; otherwise it integrates in 4 substeps, ray-tested against boards,\n   posts, nets, goalies, sticks and bodies. This emits shots, saves, posts, deflections, goals and outs.\n6. **Goalies**: positioning and the save solver (5 and 6 interleave inside the substeps).\n7. **Hits**: contacts in the check state, knockdowns, glass, grudges.\n8. **Items and hazards**.\n9. **Boom, Kaboom and sin-bin timers**.\n10. **Clock**: period end, OT, shootout.\n11. **Consumers** (they never feed back into the simulation): stats, objectives, `Talk`, `Fx`, `Crowd`, `Net`. These\n    use `RNG.fx` only.\n12. **Replay recorder**: every second step (60 Hz).\n13. **Camera director**. 14. **Rig updates** from entity state.\n\n**RNG**: the simulation draws only from `RNG.sim`, seeded per match (story: chapter number × 1000 + attempt;\nexhibition: a seed from `Date.now()` taken at setup and shown in the Dev panel; session: from `AIHub.rng('sim')` on\nthe host). Particles, crowd and commentary draw from `RNG.fx`.\n\n### 5.5 Debug handle (tests and tools read it)\n`game.debug = { snapshot(), warp(target, opts), set(key, value), cheat(name, on), event(type, data), seed(n) }`.\n`snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode, phase, period, clock, score, shots, puck: { x, y, z, owner }, skaters: [{ id,\nteam, x, y, state, ctrl }], goalies: [{ x, y, state }], boom, kaboom, fight, items, sinBin, story: { chapter, stars,\nstep }, attract: { segment, visited }, dev: { open }, net: { role, players }, errors: game.errors.length }`.\n`window.__game = { game, scenes, Match, T, Dev, Net, STORY }`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Controls (one game on every device)\n\n### 6.1 Actions and default bindings (`HOCKEY_KEYS`, built from `E.Input.DEFAULT`'s menu keys)\n| Action | DualSense | Keyboard | Mouse | With the puck | Without the puck |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| move | left stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | – | skate | skate |\n| `pass` | ✕ (Pad0) | J | right button | pass toward the aim | switch to the teammate nearest the puck; if a CPU teammate has the puck, **call for the pass** |\n| `shoot` | □ (Pad2) | K | left button | tap: wrist shot; hold: slapshot wind-up, release to fire | body check |\n| `deke` | ○ (Pad1) | L, Space | – | deke (spin move) | poke check |\n| `lob` | △ (Pad3) | I | middle button | saucer pass | hold 0.5 s near a rival: drop the gloves (or accept a challenge) |\n| `turbo` | R2 (Pad7), R1 (Pad5) | Shift | – | turbo (hold) | turbo (hold) |\n| `chirp` | L1 (Pad4) | C | – | chirp | chirp |\n| `switch` | L2 (Pad6) | Q | – | – | switch player (always) |\n| `camera` | R3 (Pad11) | V | – | cycle Broadcast / Overhead | same |\n| pause | Options | Esc, P | – | pause menu (engine) | |\n\n- **Why these keys**: WASD to move, the mouse to aim, left click to shoot and right click to pass, Shift to sprint\n  (turbo) and Space to dodge (deke) are what PC players expect from sports and action games. J/K/L/I give\n  keyboard-only players the engine's usual action cluster.\n- **Aim**: with the mouse, when the pointer moved in the last 2 s, a reticle sits on the ice at `game.mouseGround()`:\n  passes go to the teammate nearest it (within 80 u), else to the point; shots aim at the goal mouth where the line\n  through the reticle meets it. On a pad, the right stick (`input.padAim`) aims the same way while deflected. Otherwise\n  aim follows the move direction.\n- **Context reuse**: in a fight, ✕ jab, □ haymaker, ○ duck, △ yank. In the shootout when defending, the stick slides\n  the goalie, ✕ butterfly, □ poke, ○ dive. Flood Wagon: the stick steers toward that screen direction, R2/Shift is gas,\n  ✕ honks.\n- **Two on one keyboard**: `E.Input.halfKeys(HOCKEY_KEYS, 'left' | 'right')` gives P1 WASD + F G H R T Y and P2 the\n  arrows + `. / , L ; '` and the numpad, each half taking pass, shoot, deke, lob, turbo, chirp in order. The side\n  select screen shows both layouts with keycaps.\n- **Controls screen names** (`menu.controls`): pass \"Pass / Switch\", shoot \"Shoot / Check\", deke \"Deke / Poke\",\n  lob \"Saucer / Gloves\", turbo \"Turbo\", chirp \"Chirp\", switch \"Switch player\", camera \"Camera\". Hide the\n  preset's unused `attack dash skill jump` (`false`).\n\n### 6.2 Prompts and glyphs\n- `Glyphs.chip(g, action, x, y, { device })` draws the binding for the device used last (`input.lastDevice`, per\n  player). **Gamepad**: a round dark badge with the PlayStation symbol in its color (✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red\n  `#ff6b6b`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#4fe3a1`). With **Settings → Button symbols: Xbox**, the badge shows A, B, X or\n  Y in green, red, blue or yellow. Shoulders and triggers are rounded rectangles (L1 R1 L2 R2, or LB RB LT RT).\n  **Keyboard**: a keycap with the key's label. **Mouse**: a 7×10 mouse icon with the pressed button lit.\n- **Settings → Button symbols** (`PlayStation` default, `Xbox`) sets `game.input.glyphs`. Keep it in sync both ways\n  with the engine's own Controls option.\n- Text with tokens (`\"Hold {shoot} for a slapshot\"`) is laid out by `Glyphs.text(g, str, x, y, opts)`, which replaces\n  each `{action}` with a chip.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Systems (rules, numbers, behavior)\n","numLines":360,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1473}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Q9NbKy57ggDm8Lsj3axAiW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
