rix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/2-played.png","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/report.json","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/view-brawler.png","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/view-iso.png","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/view-side.png","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/view-threequarter.png","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/check-output/view-topdown.png","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183420__xf7f297/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184648__xa320fb/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-184016__x1295cd/agent.out"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011gt2Exku52y3d4k7nbPkYR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh7gbVSduuBQbvQXU9ET","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Ice Hoc-Kaboom**, an over-the-top 3-on-3 arcade hockey game, as one self-contained file: `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910.html`. The full test command passes: 181 checks, 0 failures. That covers the headless suites, the browser checks and the AI Hub harness in five configurations: 1 player, 2 players, 4 players with lag and packet loss, the host leaving, and a host with no player of its own.\n\n**What's in it:**\n- **Story (the Cup Run):** five chapters, each with its own barn and rival, cutscenes with portraits and babble voices, one choice per chapter, and three stars per chapter.\n- **Exhibition:** pick any teams and barn for 1–4 local players.\n- **Practice:** Coach Marge's seven drills.\n- **Online:** AI Hub sessions for 1–4 players.\n- **Menus and extras:**\n  - **Records and About:** the About screen carries the four report sections below.\n  - **Attract mode:** a nine-segment demo that starts after 10 seconds idle.\n  - **Dev mode:** reached from the pause menu. It has live sliders with a reset to defaults, plus cheats, warps, debug overlays, a sound test and an animation lab.\n- **Gameplay:** momentum skating with hockey stops, passes and one-timers, wrist and slap shots, and goalies that react and give rebounds. Checks can crack and shatter the glass. Teams fill a BOOM meter to go KABOOM, fights send the loser to the sin bin, and drawn games go to overtime and a shootout. Fans throw power-ups, and some barns add thin ice, octopi or a mascot. Goals get instant replays, and a booth crew calls the game. Between periods you can drive the Flood Wagon.\n- **Controls:** prompts follow whichever device you used last, with PlayStation symbols by default and an Xbox option in Settings. On a touch-only device it shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button, and the demo still runs.\n- **Balance:** in AI-vs-AI test games there are about 5–13 goals a game with a save rate around .86.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- The story test proves the game can be won, not that it's easy. The bot plays one real period with a strong team against a weak one. For the later periods it skips the clock forward and adds a goal if needed. When a chapter's key event hasn't happened by itself, the test forces it with a dev warp.\n- The drill tests mostly set up their conditions directly (placing the skater at the gates, for example), so they show each drill can be completed rather than that it plays well.\n- The AI Hub harness only proves the protocol. I haven't played a session against real people on separate machines.\n- I followed TASK.md's file names; the plan used the max-effort run's names.\n- I made five small engine changes, listed in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` and on the About screen.\n\nRun `node tests/run.mjs` in the dev folder to repeat the tests; `--quick` skips the browser and hub checks.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:29:10\n- **Source:** Ice hockey (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports: 3-on-3 hockey with goalies, a fight minigame, a five-chapter story, exhibition, practice drills and 1–4 player versus. The camera is a 2.5D TV broadcast view (the engine's threequarter view) that tracks the puck. An Overhead (topdown) option is in Settings, and an ice-level brawler view takes over for fights, replays and player intros.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the full engine with five patches. Plain JavaScript in one HTML file, using the AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Tests use Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium).\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's own PS1-era pixel art with its detailed humanoid characters. On top of that, everything hockey is drawn in code: sticks, skates, helmets with visors, goalie gear, jersey numbers, team logos, five dressed arenas with fans and breakable glass, and effects.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character rigs. I added skating strides, crossovers, hockey stops, stick handling, shots, checks, pokes, deke spins, goalie stances and saves, and celebrations. Fights and reactions use the engine's own punches and poses.\n- **Audio:** All made in code, with no audio files. Chiptune music (title theme, locker room, pond, each barn, the final, a gameplay track whose extra layers kick in for KABOOM, fights and late periods, a Flood Wagon waltz, victory, defeat, credits), organ stings, sound effects (post ping, slapshot, glass shatter, goal horn and more), crowd noise, and gibberish voices for each character.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, using the game's own pre-round card. The host runs the match, each client sees the others' moves smoothed out, and its own skater responds immediately. Plus local 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node suites drive the game with `game.advance` and read it with `game.debug`. They cover units, systems, 4 local players, determinism, dev mode, attract mode, all 7 drills, AI balance, a scripted run through the whole story with a bot, and text pools. Browser checks cover pages, menus, mouse hover and click, button prompts and touch-only mode, plus the AI Hub harness in 5 configurations. All of it runs from one command, and I reviewed screenshots by eye.\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff docs and parts of its source, and the AI Hub protocol and harness. No web research on hockey.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 25. The main ones:\n  - a naming clash that crashed the browser build (it only showed up in a browser);\n  - skaters unable to shoot during the shootout;\n  - AI shooters losing their wind-up during hockey stops;\n  - goalies saving nearly everything, and cross-crease one-timers never scoring;\n  - too many hockey stops and too many KABOOMs;\n  - edge cases in the drills and faceoffs;\n  - a button press swallowed during the hit-stop freeze;\n  - chapter 4's showcase shootout not starting;\n  - several mistakes in my own test setups.\n- **How many would be considered critical?** Four: the browser crash, the shootout that never ended, goals almost never happening, and a faceoff crash when a team had nobody on the ice.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted run through the whole story together with the AI-vs-AI balance games, because between them they exercise everything end to end.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The engine check tool's \"too much flat color\" notes. A hockey rink is mostly bright ice, so those notes were noise.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** The procedural characters with live dialog portraits, and the fixed-step headless mode, which made testing fast and repeatable.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - At zoom 1 the threequarter view was far too close for a rink, because of its built-in 1.5 scale.\n  - The hit-stop freeze swallows button presses.\n  - Each zoom change re-renders the floor, so smooth zooming wasn't practical.\n  - Placing scaled sprites took trial and error.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hockey simulation: skating physics, the puck against curved boards, posts and nets, and the goalie save logic. Also the arena drawing.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Getting boards, glass, nets and stands to layer correctly in front of and behind players, while keeping floor rendering cheap.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n  - PlayStation button symbols added to the pixel font, because they showed as \"?\".\n  - The Options button is ignored while Create is held, because AI Hub reserves that combination.\n  - A hook so games can draw their own gear on characters (sticks, goalie pads, numbers) in the right layer.\n  - Organ and brass sound waves, a lowpass filter and a separate voice volume, for the organ, the goal horn and the voices.\n  - Dialog can call a function per letter, so each speaker babbles in their own voice.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Built-in support for held items such as sticks, bats and rackets, beyond the current sword, gun and staff.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** Splitting what to build (TASK.md) from how to build it (the plan), and the concrete definition of done.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan's file names used the max-effort run's suffix, which conflicted with TASK.md (TASK.md won). The Online line pointed at the hub library without naming a mode.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting the whole feature list into one coherent, tested build.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Say whether a plan written at max effort should be trimmed for a lower effort level, and give a time or usage budget.\n\nFiles are in the run's `work` folder:\n- `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910.html` (the game)\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/` (dev folder: `src/`, `tests/`, `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":622654,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":757,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2adef66c-4a6d-4909-bb60-db906fac5610","uuid":"d358fa56-28c2-44c8-b221-54e19e84630e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:41:47.366Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh7gbEKZLM9B7EEt9gBK"}
