{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghDhnVxBNGma5S1ZmsBZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The chapter 1 one-timer star is now earned through ordinary play. A test bot plays like someone chasing that star: it feeds the open man across the crease and shoots first-time when a pass comes. It scored one-timer goals in 2 of 3 full games (4 in one, 2 in another), so with stars kept across retries the star comes within three attempts. The full test command passes on the final build: 183 checks, 0 failures.\n\n**Online play is built as TASK.md asks.** TASK.md says to follow the AI Hub library for multiplayer, and the game does: it shows its own card before the round, the host runs the match while clients send their input and draw the host's game, a player who leaves is taken over by the computer, and results are reported to the hub. It passes the hub's official harness with 1, 2 and 4 players, with lag and packet loss, when the host leaves, and with a host that has no player of its own; that harness is how the hub decides a game works.\n\nI also ran a session between two copies of the game in which real key presses on the non-host copy drive that player's skater in the host's game. That session is how I found and fixed a bug where remote players' input never reached their skaters.\n\n**Not done: people playing on separate machines.** That is a human playtest rather than a feature, and I can't arrange it from here.\n\nThe single HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in the folder root. The JSON parses with every field filled, and the game contains no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:29:10\n- **Source:** Ice hockey (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports: 3-on-3 hockey with goalies, a fight minigame, a 5-chapter story, exhibition, practice drills and 1–4 player versus. The camera is a TV-broadcast style view that follows the puck, with an Overhead option in Settings and an ice-level view for fights, replays and player intros.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, with 5 small changes), plain JavaScript in one HTML file, AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Tests use Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 with Chromium.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's PS1-era pixel art and detailed characters. Hockey gear, team logos, five arenas with breakable glass, lit textured ice and the effects are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural characters, plus new hockey motion: skating, stops, stick handling, shots, checks, dekes, goalie stances and celebrations. Fights use the engine's own punches and poses.\n- **Audio:** Everything is made in code: chiptune music with organ and brass, sound effects, the goal horn, crowd noise and gibberish voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 1–4 players, run on the host's copy, with the game's own card. Plus 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** One command, `node tests/run.mjs`, runs:\n  - headless test suites that drive the game directly;\n  - all 7 drills played through controller input only;\n  - each chapter played without forcing any events;\n  - browser checks;\n  - the AI Hub harness in 5 configurations;\n  - a two-copy hub session driven by real key presses.\n  - I also reviewed screenshots by eye.\n- **Research:** The engine's docs and source, and the AI Hub protocol and harness. No web research on hockey.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35; the list is in `exit-interview.json`.\n- **How many were critical?** Five:\n  - remote players' input never reaching their skaters online;\n  - a crash in the browser;\n  - a shootout that never ended;\n  - almost no goals in AI-vs-AI games;\n  - a faceoff crash when a team had no skaters on the ice.\n- **Most helpful test:** The two-copy hub session driven by real key presses. It caught the remote-input bug, which the official harness checklist did not.\n- **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed always gives the same game. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** The procedural characters with live portraits, and the fixed-step headless mode that makes tests fast.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - The broadcast view starts far too zoomed in for a rink.\n  - The hit-stop freeze swallows button presses.\n  - Every zoom change re-draws the floor.\n  - Placing scaled sprites took trial and error.\n- **What did you have to build yourself?** The hockey simulation and the arena drawing.\n- **What was hardest?** Getting the boards, glass and nets to layer correctly in front of and behind the players.\n- **What did you change in the engine?**\n  - PlayStation button symbols in the font.\n  - The Options button ignored while Create is held, as the hub requires.\n  - A hook for drawing gear on characters.\n  - Organ and brass sounds, a lowpass filter, and a separate voice volume.\n  - Per-letter voices in dialog.\n- **One thing you'd change:** Built-in support for held items such as sticks.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** TASK.md says what to build and the plan says how, with clear criteria for done.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan used the max-effort run's file names (I followed TASK.md). The Online section doesn't name a session mode.\n- **What was hardest?** Getting the full feature list into one tested build.\n- **What would you change?** Say whether a max-effort plan should be trimmed for lower effort levels, and give a time or usage budget."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":791,"cache_read_input_tokens":702180,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_inp